1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,171
This is ECE 358, video 3. The topic is network performance: delay, loss, and throughput.

2
00:00:08,621 --> 00:00:13,634
After this video, you can name and calculate the four types of packet delay.

3
00:00:14,084 --> 00:00:18,137
You can use the traffic intensity to judge the queueing delay.

4
00:00:18,587 --> 00:00:21,595
You can find the throughput from the bottleneck link.

5
00:00:22,045 --> 00:00:29,405
And you can solve four problems from Problem Set 1, and the question bank questions 32 to 43.

6
00:00:29,855 --> 00:00:32,671
Hosts A and B send packets to one router.

7
00:00:33,121 --> 00:00:38,924
The router sends the packets out on one output link. The link rate is R bits per second.

8
00:00:39,374 --> 00:00:47,779
The router transmits one packet at a time. The time to push one packet onto the link is the transmission delay, d trans.

9
00:00:48,229 --> 00:00:52,475
Sometimes packets arrive faster than the output link can send them.

10
00:00:52,925 --> 00:00:57,703
Then the packets wait in the router's buffer. The buffer is memory in the router.

11
00:00:58,153 --> 00:01:01,823
The waiting time in the buffer is the queueing delay, d queue.

12
00:01:02,273 --> 00:01:12,513
The queue length is the number of packets that wait. It grows while the arrival rate stays above the link rate. Now all 8 places of the buffer are full.

13
00:01:12,963 --> 00:01:17,805
A packet that arrives at a full buffer is dropped. This is packet loss.

14
00:01:18,255 --> 00:01:25,274
So delay and loss have the same cause. For some time, the arrival rate is above the output link rate.

15
00:01:25,724 --> 00:01:31,569
A packet X goes from one router to the next router. Four delays add up on this hop.

16
00:01:32,019 --> 00:01:36,819
At time t 1, packet X arrives at the input of the first router.

17
00:01:37,269 --> 00:01:46,187
At time t 2, packet X arrives at the input of the next router. The time from t 1 to t 2 is cut into four parts.

18
00:01:46,637 --> 00:01:57,602
First, nodal processing, d proc. The router checks the packet for bit errors, and it determines the output link. This typically takes less than a microsecond.

19
00:01:58,052 --> 00:02:09,636
Second, queueing delay, d queue. The packet waits at the output link for its turn. It depends on the congestion level of the router. It can be zero, or very large.

20
00:02:10,086 --> 00:02:23,206
Third, transmission delay, d trans. The router must push all bits of the packet onto the link. A packet of L bits on a link of rate R bits per second takes L over R seconds.

21
00:02:23,656 --> 00:02:34,707
Fourth, propagation delay, d prop. One bit must travel the length of the link. A link of length d, with propagation speed s, takes d over s seconds.

22
00:02:35,157 --> 00:02:47,893
t 2 minus t 1 is the nodal delay. d nodal equals d proc, plus d queue, plus d trans, plus d prop. Learn this equation. Most delay questions start from it.

23
00:02:48,343 --> 00:02:54,700
This diagram is a space-time diagram. Distance runs across, and time runs down.

24
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:59,673
At time 0, the sender puts the first bit of the packet on the link.

25
00:03:00,123 --> 00:03:06,245
The first bit travels at speed s. It arrives at the receiver after d over s seconds.

26
00:03:06,695 --> 00:03:13,159
This is the propagation delay, d prop. It depends only on the link length d and the medium.

27
00:03:13,609 --> 00:03:17,065
The sender pushes one bit every 1 over R seconds.

28
00:03:17,515 --> 00:03:27,883
The packet has L bits. So the last bit leaves after L times 1 over R: L over R seconds. The blue band is the packet on the link.

29
00:03:28,333 --> 00:03:34,627
This is the transmission delay, d trans. It depends only on the packet length and the link rate.

30
00:03:35,077 --> 00:03:42,479
The last bit arrives at L over R plus d over s. Only then does the receiver have the full packet.

31
00:03:42,929 --> 00:03:49,628
Do not mix up the two delays. d trans depends on the rate. d prop depends on the distance.

32
00:03:50,078 --> 00:03:56,009
The propagation speed s in a guided medium is about two thirds of c, the speed of light.

33
00:03:56,459 --> 00:04:01,813
In free space, c is about 3 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second.

34
00:04:02,263 --> 00:04:06,573
So s is about 2 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second.

35
00:04:07,023 --> 00:04:24,196
On a university campus, d is 100 meters, which is 10 to the power 2 meters. 10 to the 2, divided by 2 times 10 to the 8, is 0.5 times 10 to the minus 6 seconds. That is 0.5 microseconds.

36
00:04:24,646 --> 00:04:40,859
Toronto to Montreal is about 500 kilometers, which is 5 times 10 to the 5 meters. Divided by 2 times 10 to the 8, this is 2.5 times 10 to the minus 3 seconds: 2.5 milliseconds.

37
00:04:41,309 --> 00:04:56,947
London to Tokyo is about 12,000 kilometers, which is 1.2 times 10 to the 7 meters. Divided by 2 times 10 to the 8, this is 6 times 10 to the minus 2 seconds: 60 milliseconds.

38
00:04:57,397 --> 00:05:02,943
The time axis is logarithmic. Each grid line is ten times the one before it.

39
00:05:03,393 --> 00:05:15,916
Here is an example with our own numbers. A packet of 1500 bytes crosses a link of 500 kilometers. Compare the two delays. Pause the video and try it.

40
00:05:16,366 --> 00:05:23,939
First, write L in bits. One byte is 8 bits, so 1500 bytes is 12,000 bits.

41
00:05:24,389 --> 00:05:40,283
At 10 megabits per second, R is 10 to the power 7 bits per second. d trans is 12,000 divided by 10 to the 7. That is 1.2 times 10 to the minus 3 seconds: 1.2 milliseconds.

42
00:05:40,733 --> 00:05:51,506
At 1 gigabit per second, R is 10 to the power 9. d trans is 12,000 divided by 10 to the 9. That is 12 microseconds.

43
00:05:51,956 --> 00:06:03,796
d prop for 500 kilometers is 5 times 10 to the 5, divided by 2 times 10 to the 8: 2.5 milliseconds. It is the same at any rate.

44
00:06:04,246 --> 00:06:12,097
On the fast link, the 2.5 milliseconds of d prop are much more than the 12 microseconds of d trans.

45
00:06:12,547 --> 00:06:20,163
On the slower link, d trans is larger: 1.2 milliseconds. So always calculate both delays.

46
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:27,717
Now look at the queueing delay more closely. Packets arrive at an average rate a, in packets per second.

47
00:06:28,167 --> 00:06:30,001
Each packet has L bits.

48
00:06:30,451 --> 00:06:33,545
So bits arrive at L times a bits per second.

49
00:06:33,995 --> 00:06:36,384
The link sends R bits per second.

50
00:06:36,834 --> 00:06:45,559
The ratio L a over R is the traffic intensity. It is the arrival rate of bits, divided by the service rate of bits.

51
00:06:46,009 --> 00:06:55,375
The graph shows the average queueing delay against the traffic intensity. It is not linear. It grows slowly at first, and then very fast.

52
00:06:55,825 --> 00:07:04,273
When L a over R is near 0, few bits arrive. A packet seldom finds a queue. The average queueing delay is small.

53
00:07:04,723 --> 00:07:18,440
When L a over R goes near 1, the average queueing delay becomes large. Packets arrive in bursts, not at a steady rate. A burst fills the queue, and the link has no spare time to empty it.

54
00:07:18,890 --> 00:07:32,053
When L a over R is more than 1, more work arrives than the link can serve. The queue grows without limit, and the average delay is infinite. So keep the traffic intensity below 1.

55
00:07:32,503 --> 00:07:41,591
The traceroute program measures real delays. It measures the round trip delay from the source to each router on the path to the destination.

56
00:07:42,041 --> 00:07:50,766
For router 1, the sender sends three packets, called probes, at time t 1. Their time-to-live field, TTL, is 1.

57
00:07:51,216 --> 00:08:01,541
Router 1 returns the probes. They arrive back at the sender at time t 2. t 2 minus t 1 is the round trip delay to router 1.

58
00:08:01,991 --> 00:08:07,666
For router 2, the sender sets the TTL to 2. Router 2 returns the probes.

59
00:08:08,116 --> 00:08:16,521
This continues for router 3, router 4, and so on, to the destination. Each router gives 3 delay measurements.

60
00:08:16,971 --> 00:08:23,670
Here is part of a real traceroute, from the University of Massachusetts to a host in France.

61
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:30,200
Router 1 is on the same campus. Its three delays are 1, 1, and 2 milliseconds.

62
00:08:30,650 --> 00:08:36,367
Router 7 is in the United States. Its three delays are 22 milliseconds.

63
00:08:36,817 --> 00:08:48,380
At router 8, the delays jump to more than 100 milliseconds. Between routers 7 and 8 is the trans-oceanic link. Its propagation delay is large.

64
00:08:48,830 --> 00:08:59,006
Delays can also decrease. Router 11 has smaller delays than router 10. The reason is that queueing delays change from one probe to the next.

65
00:08:59,456 --> 00:09:05,088
A star means no response. The probe was lost, or the router did not reply.

66
00:09:05,538 --> 00:09:08,823
The buffer in front of a link has a finite capacity.

67
00:09:09,273 --> 00:09:15,140
A packet that arrives at a full buffer is dropped. We also say that the packet is lost.

68
00:09:15,590 --> 00:09:22,033
There are three possible results for the lost packet. First, the previous node can retransmit it.

69
00:09:22,483 --> 00:09:25,469
Second, the source end system can retransmit it.

70
00:09:25,919 --> 00:09:28,053
Third, nobody retransmits it.

71
00:09:28,503 --> 00:09:33,537
The protocol decides. Later chapters show which protocols retransmit.

72
00:09:33,987 --> 00:09:42,350
Throughput is the rate, in bits per time unit, at which the receiver gets bits from the sender. Here the receiver is the client.

73
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:45,744
The server sends a file of F bits to the client.

74
00:09:46,194 --> 00:09:51,015
The bits go through two links, with rates R s and R c bits per second.

75
00:09:51,465 --> 00:09:58,676
The instantaneous throughput is the rate at one point in time, over a short period. It changes with time.

76
00:09:59,126 --> 00:10:07,446
The average throughput is the rate over a longer period. For a file, it is F bits divided by the total transfer time.

77
00:10:07,896 --> 00:10:10,947
Case 1: R s is smaller than R c.

78
00:10:11,397 --> 00:10:21,253
The server link carries only R s bits per second. The client link cannot get more bits than the server link gives it. So the throughput is R s.

79
00:10:21,703 --> 00:10:24,711
Case 2: R s is larger than R c.

80
00:10:25,161 --> 00:10:34,398
The server pushes bits fast, but the client link carries only R c. Bits queue in front of the client link. So the throughput is R c.

81
00:10:34,848 --> 00:10:38,859
In both cases, the throughput is the minimum of the two rates.

82
00:10:39,309 --> 00:10:44,962
The link that sets this minimum is the bottleneck link. It limits the end-to-end throughput.

83
00:10:45,412 --> 00:10:50,191
Now 10 servers send to 10 clients. The figure draws 3 of each.

84
00:10:50,641 --> 00:10:54,459
All 10 connections cross one backbone link with rate R.

85
00:10:54,909 --> 00:11:00,072
The connections share the backbone fairly. So each connection gets R over 10.

86
00:11:00,522 --> 00:11:07,093
Each connection also uses its own server link, R s, and its own client link, R c.

87
00:11:07,543 --> 00:11:13,473
So the per-connection throughput is the minimum of R c, R s, and R over 10.

88
00:11:13,923 --> 00:11:20,793
In practice, R c or R s is often the bottleneck, because the backbone rate is very high.

89
00:11:21,243 --> 00:11:31,547
A short check with our own numbers. R s is 2 megabits per second, R c is 1 megabit per second, and R is 5 megabits per second.

90
00:11:31,997 --> 00:11:37,821
R over 10 is 5 divided by 10: 0.5 megabits per second.

91
00:11:38,271 --> 00:11:50,239
The minimum of 1, 2, and 0.5 is 0.5 megabits per second, which is 500 kilobits per second. Here the backbone is the bottleneck.

92
00:11:50,689 --> 00:11:59,969
Now the question bank. These are short descriptive questions. Pause the video and try them. The answers come from the slides of this video.

93
00:12:00,419 --> 00:12:12,877
Question 32. Delay is the time a packet needs to reach its destination. Loss is the dropping of packets at full buffers. Throughput is the rate at which the receiver gets bits.

94
00:12:13,327 --> 00:12:19,941
Question 33. The four delays are processing, queueing, transmission, and propagation.

95
00:12:20,391 --> 00:12:28,497
Question 34. Two router functions cause the processing delay: check for bit errors, and determine the output link.

96
00:12:28,947 --> 00:12:36,137
Question 35. A packet waits because the arrival rate is above the output link rate for some time.

97
00:12:36,587 --> 00:12:44,501
Question 36. Transmission delay exists because the router must push all L bits onto the link at rate R.

98
00:12:44,951 --> 00:12:49,858
Question 37. The physical link causes the propagation delay.

99
00:12:50,308 --> 00:13:02,575
Question 38. In free space, signals travel at about 3 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second. In guided media, about 2 times 10 to the power 8.

100
00:13:03,025 --> 00:13:07,313
Questions 39 to 43. Pause the video and try them.

101
00:13:07,763 --> 00:13:16,445
Question 39. Traffic intensity is L a over R: the arrival rate of bits divided by the service rate of bits.

102
00:13:16,895 --> 00:13:32,490
Question 40. When the input rate equals the output rate, L a over R is 1. Packets arrive in bursts. A burst fills the queue, and the link has no spare capacity to empty it. So the queueing delay is very high.

103
00:13:32,940 --> 00:13:47,745
Question 41. A transcontinental round trip delay is one to two hundred milliseconds. In the traceroute example, routers 8 to 19 had delays from 102 to 136 milliseconds.

104
00:13:48,195 --> 00:13:58,201
Question 42. Instantaneous throughput is the rate at one point in time. Long-term average throughput is the rate over a longer period.

105
00:13:58,651 --> 00:14:12,368
Question 43. The bottleneck link is the link with the smallest rate on the path. The end-to-end throughput equals its rate. So to analyze the throughput for a receiving host, you find this one link.

106
00:14:12,818 --> 00:14:27,154
Problem 5 of Problem Set 1. N packets arrive at the same time at an empty link. Each packet has L bits, and the link rate is R. Find the average queueing delay. Pause the video and try it.

107
00:14:27,604 --> 00:14:37,183
The link sends one packet at a time. The first packet starts at once. Its queueing delay is 0. Its transmission takes L over R.

108
00:14:37,633 --> 00:14:42,774
The second packet waits for the first packet. Its queueing delay is L over R.

109
00:14:43,224 --> 00:14:48,429
The third packet waits for two packets. Its queueing delay is 2 L over R.

110
00:14:48,879 --> 00:15:01,039
The picture uses N equal to 4. Packet 4 waits for three packets: 3 L over R. In general, packet n waits n minus 1, times L over R.

111
00:15:01,489 --> 00:15:09,895
Add the four waiting times. 0 plus 1 plus 2 plus 3 is 6. So the total wait is 6 L over R.

112
00:15:10,345 --> 00:15:17,214
Divide by the 4 packets. 6 L over R, divided by 4, is 1.5 L over R.

113
00:15:17,664 --> 00:15:25,557
In general, the average is 0 plus 1, up to N minus 1, divided by N, times L over R.

114
00:15:26,007 --> 00:15:39,234
The sum 0 plus 1 up to N minus 1 is N times N minus 1, over 2. Divide by N. So the average queueing delay is N minus 1, times L, over 2 R.

115
00:15:39,684 --> 00:15:47,769
Check with N equal to 4. 3 L over 2 R is 1.5 L over R. This agrees with the picture.

116
00:15:48,219 --> 00:16:06,075
Problem 6 of Problem Set 1. Host A and host B are 10,000 kilometers apart. The link rate is 1 gigabit per second, and the propagation speed is 2.5 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second. Pause the video and try it.

117
00:16:06,525 --> 00:16:15,549
First, write d in meters. 10,000 kilometers is 10 to the 4, times 10 to the 3 meters: 10 to the 7 meters.

118
00:16:15,999 --> 00:16:26,538
The propagation delay is d over s. 10 to the 7, divided by 2.5 times 10 to the 8, is 0.04 seconds.

119
00:16:26,988 --> 00:16:41,452
Part a. R is 10 to the 9 bits per second. R times d prop is 10 to the 9 times 0.04: 4 times 10 to the 7 bits. This is the bandwidth-delay product.

120
00:16:41,902 --> 00:16:45,806
It is the number of bits in the link when the link is full of bits.

121
00:16:46,256 --> 00:16:59,483
Part b. The file has only 400,000 bits: 4 times 10 to the 5. This is less than 4 times 10 to the 7. The file fills only one hundredth of the link.

122
00:16:59,933 --> 00:17:16,423
A sends the file in 4 times 10 to the 5, divided by 10 to the 9 seconds: 0.4 milliseconds. This is less than d prop, 40 milliseconds. So the last bit leaves A before the first bit arrives at B.

123
00:17:16,873 --> 00:17:21,247
So the maximum number of bits in the link is 400,000.

124
00:17:21,697 --> 00:17:37,398
Part c. The link is 10 to the 7 meters long. When full, it holds 4 times 10 to the 7 bits. So one bit is 10 to the 7, divided by 4 times 10 to the 7: 0.25 meters wide.

125
00:17:37,848 --> 00:17:45,101
This also equals s over R: 2.5 times 10 to the 8, divided by 10 to the 9.

126
00:17:45,551 --> 00:17:59,098
Problem 1 of Problem Set 1. A and B are connected by n links through n minus 1 routers. Each link has capacity C. Propagation delay is zero. Pause the video and try it.

127
00:17:59,548 --> 00:18:07,441
Part a. The diagram shows n equal to 3 links. A sends one packet on link 1. This takes L over C.

128
00:18:07,891 --> 00:18:17,555
A router must receive the full packet before it sends it on. This is store-and-forward. So each of the next links adds one more L over C.

129
00:18:18,005 --> 00:18:23,701
With n links, one packet takes n times L over C. Here, 3 L over C.

130
00:18:24,151 --> 00:18:33,730
Part b. Now the file of S bits is cut into k packets. Each packet carries S over k data bits, plus a header of H bits.

131
00:18:34,180 --> 00:18:38,489
So one packet time is S over k plus H, all over C.

132
00:18:38,939 --> 00:18:46,342
With k packets, the links work in parallel. While packet 2 is on link 1, packet 1 is on link 2.

133
00:18:46,792 --> 00:18:50,419
The first packet arrives at B after n packet times.

134
00:18:50,869 --> 00:19:02,069
Then one more packet arrives in each packet time. So k packets need n plus k minus 1 packet times. Here, 3 plus 3 minus 1 is 5.

135
00:19:02,519 --> 00:19:08,385
So T of k is n plus k minus 1, times S over k plus H, over C.

136
00:19:08,835 --> 00:19:15,897
Start from T of k. Multiply it by C. The curve shows C times T of k for one example.

137
00:19:16,347 --> 00:19:25,627
Multiply out. You get n minus 1 times S over k, plus n minus 1 times H, plus S, plus k times H.

138
00:19:26,077 --> 00:19:39,026
Take the derivative with respect to k, and set it to zero. The two terms without k disappear. You get minus n minus 1 times S over k squared, plus H, equals zero.

139
00:19:39,476 --> 00:19:47,689
Solve for k. The best number of packets, k star, is the square root of n minus 1, times S, over H.

140
00:19:48,139 --> 00:19:56,502
k star is usually not an integer. So evaluate T at the integer below and the integer above, and take the better one.

141
00:19:56,952 --> 00:20:03,949
The plot uses our own numbers: S is 10,000 bits, H is 100 bits, and n is 3.

142
00:20:04,399 --> 00:20:13,807
Then k star is the square root of 2 times 10,000 over 100. That is the square root of 200, which is 14.1.

143
00:20:14,257 --> 00:20:25,564
C times T of 14 is 13,029 bits. C times T of 15 is 13,033 bits. So the best k is 14.

144
00:20:26,014 --> 00:20:35,081
This is the trade-off. With a small k, there are few large packets. The links work less in parallel, so the curve is high on the left.

145
00:20:35,531 --> 00:20:42,272
With a large k, there are many small packets. The header bits add up, so the curve rises on the right.

146
00:20:42,722 --> 00:20:58,402
Problem 3 of Problem Set 1. Host A sends a large file to host B over three links. The rates are 500 kilobits per second, 2 megabits per second, and 1 megabit per second. Pause the video and try it.

147
00:20:58,852 --> 00:21:05,572
The first link, with 500 kilobits per second, has the smallest rate. So it is the bottleneck.

148
00:21:06,022 --> 00:21:13,787
The file has 4 million bytes. Times 8, this is 3.2 times 10 to the 7 bits. Call it M.

149
00:21:14,237 --> 00:21:24,264
Case 1, the fluid case. The bits flow without a stop through all three links. The throughput is the minimum rate, 500 kilobits per second.

150
00:21:24,714 --> 00:21:36,746
The time is M over 500 kilobits per second. 3.2 times 10 to the 7, divided by 5 times 10 to the 5, is 64 seconds.

151
00:21:37,196 --> 00:21:48,076
Case 2, message switching. The whole file is one message. Each router must receive all of it before it sends it on. So the three transmission times add.

152
00:21:48,526 --> 00:22:00,430
M over R 2 is 16 seconds, and M over R 3 is 32 seconds. 64 plus 16 plus 32 is 112 seconds.

153
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:16,005
The throughput is M over this time. 3.2 times 10 to the 7, divided by 112, is about 285.7 kilobits per second. This is much less than 500.

154
00:22:16,455 --> 00:22:23,261
Case 3, packet switching. The file is cut into K small packets. The links work in parallel.

155
00:22:23,711 --> 00:22:38,815
Only the bottleneck time K L over R 1, which is M over R 1, is large. So the time is about 64 seconds, and the throughput is about R 1, 500 kilobits per second.

156
00:22:39,265 --> 00:22:46,347
With message switching, link 1 first sends the whole message: M over R 1, 64 seconds.

157
00:22:46,797 --> 00:22:51,917
Only then can link 2 start. It takes M over R 2, 16 seconds.

158
00:22:52,367 --> 00:23:01,157
Then link 3 takes M over R 3, 32 seconds. The times are in series. The total is 112 seconds.

159
00:23:01,607 --> 00:23:11,441
With packet switching, link 1 sends K packets. This takes K L over R 1, which is M over R 1: 64 seconds.

160
00:23:11,891 --> 00:23:28,510
Links 2 and 3 forward each packet while link 1 sends the next packet. Only the last packet adds L over R 2, plus L over R 3. With small packets, this is very short. So the total is about 64 seconds.

161
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:48,864
The published solution gives 285 kilobits per second and 112.3 seconds. It rounds the throughput down to 285 thousand first. 3.2 times 10 to the 7, divided by 285 thousand, is 112.3.

162
00:23:49,314 --> 00:23:56,311
The exact values are 285.7 kilobits per second and 112 seconds.

163
00:23:56,761 --> 00:24:00,623
To recap. The nodal delay is the sum of four delays.

164
00:24:01,073 --> 00:24:06,534
Transmission delay is L over R. Propagation delay is d over s.

165
00:24:06,984 --> 00:24:12,808
Traffic intensity L a over R controls the queueing delay. Keep it below 1.

166
00:24:13,258 --> 00:24:16,245
A packet that arrives at a full buffer is lost.

167
00:24:16,695 --> 00:24:23,457
Throughput is the rate of the bottleneck link. When N connections share a link, each gets R over N.

168
00:24:23,907 --> 00:24:33,401
With store-and-forward, one packet over n links takes n L over R, and k packets take n plus k minus 1 packet times.

169
00:24:33,851 --> 00:24:45,968
N packets that arrive together have an average queueing delay of N minus 1, times L, over 2 R. The next video covers protocol layers and encapsulation.
